15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"August 1861–March 11, 1862; main battle February 7–8, 1862","place":"Hatteras Inlet, Pamlico Sound, Croatan Sound, and Roanoke Island, North Carolina","result":"United States Army and Navy forces captured Roanoke Island on February 8, 1862. Burnside landed about 7,500 soldiers south of the Confederate forts, attacked the central road redoubt, and sent brigades through the swamps around both flanks. Colonel Henry Shaw surrendered the island and about 2,675 men. The victory opened Albemarle Sound and eastern North Carolina to further United States operations","facts":[{"label":"Roanoke Island","value":"About 12 miles long and 3 miles wide"},{"label":"Burnside expedition","value":"More than 15,000 soldiers and over 80 vessels assembled"},{"label":"United States troops landed","value":"About 7,500 by midnight on February 7"},{"label":"Confederate defenders","value":"About 2,500–3,000 on the island"},{"label":"Confederate fixed defenses","value":"Three coastal forts, one central road redoubt, and the floating Fort Forrest"},{"label":"United States losses stated in episode","value":"Army: 37 killed, 214 wounded; Navy: 3 killed, 11 wounded"},{"label":"Confederate losses stated in episode","value":"23 killed, 58 wounded, 2,675 captured"},{"label":"Result","value":"United States victory; Roanoke Island and access to Albemarle Sound secured"}],"maps":[{"title":"The North Carolina sounds","caption":"Roanoke Island separates Croatan Sound from Roanoke Sound and stands between Pamlico Sound to the south and Albemarle Sound to the north. Hatteras Inlet gave Burnside an entrance from the Atlantic.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-76.50%2C34.70%2C-74.75%2C36.30&layer=mapnik&marker=35.891%2C-75.661"},{"title":"Roanoke Island today","caption":"The expedition landed near Ashby’s Harbor on the west side of the island, moved north along the only main road, and encountered the Confederate earthwork at the island’s narrow center.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-75.82%2C35.75%2C-75.49%2C36.05&layer=mapnik&marker=35.891%2C-75.661"},{"title":"Plan of the Battle of Roanoke Island","caption":"Robert Knox Sneden’s wartime map shows the February 7 landing, the road, the swamp-flanked Confederate redoubt, the three United States brigade routes, and the forts and naval positions in Croatan Sound.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00070/","image":"/images/roanoke-island-battle-map.jpg","alt":"Color wartime map of the Battle of Roanoke Island showing United States and Confederate positions, forts, roads, landing site, and troop movements"},{"title":"The expedition’s next objectives","caption":"After Roanoke Island, Burnside planned to move south across Pamlico Sound to New Bern, then take Fort Macon at Beaufort. These operations threatened the coast and the rail connections supporting Confederate forces in Virginia.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.35%2C34.55%2C-75.15%2C36.20&layer=mapnik&marker=35.108%2C-77.044"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:09","audioTime":69,"title":"Hatteras Inlet provided the base","detail":"United States forces captured the inlet’s forts in August 1861 and retained the position as a blockading base and supply depot."},{"time":"2:02","audioTime":122,"title":"The sounds exposed coastal North Carolina","detail":"Control of Hatteras Inlet opened Pamlico Sound. Roanoke Island still blocked access to Albemarle Sound and the rivers and towns beyond it."},{"time":"2:27","audioTime":147,"title":"McClellan and Burnside planned a coastal force","detail":"Both later claimed the Roanoke Island idea. On September 12, McClellan ordered Burnside to recruit New England troops familiar with ships, surf boats, and coastal work."},{"time":"3:24","audioTime":204,"title":"The division needed its own shallow-draft transport","detail":"Burnside was told to obtain vessels able to carry troops, mount heavy guns, and operate in the bays, rivers, and shallow sounds of Virginia and North Carolina."},{"time":"4:31","audioTime":271,"title":"McClellan became general in chief","detail":"Winfield Scott retired on November 1, 1861. McClellan then commanded the Army of the Potomac and directed all United States armies."},{"time":"5:00","audioTime":300,"title":"Rush Hawkins argued for a larger operation","detail":"Hawkins, commanding at Hatteras, urged the War Department to reinforce the position and attack the North Carolina coast."},{"time":"5:42","audioTime":342,"title":"The cabinet approved the coastal campaign","detail":"Hawkins presented the case in Washington. McClellan and Navy Secretary Gideon Welles supported further operations."},{"time":"6:06","audioTime":366,"title":"The campaign had several military objectives","detail":"A coastal force could threaten Confederate troops in Virginia, disrupt the Wilmington and Weldon Railroad, seize blockade-running ports, and free naval vessels for other duties."},{"time":"6:43","audioTime":403,"title":"Lincoln expected support from North Carolina Unionists","detail":"The administration hoped occupation would help organize a loyal state government and return North Carolina to the United States."},{"time":"7:42","audioTime":462,"title":"Burnside and Goldsborough received the joint mission","detail":"Burnside commanded the army component. Louis M. Goldsborough, head of the North Atlantic Blockading Squadron, commanded the naval force."},{"time":"8:10","audioTime":490,"title":"McClellan set the objectives","detail":"Orders dated January 7 called for Roanoke Island, New Bern, and Fort Macon. A later advance might threaten Raleigh and the Wilmington and Weldon Railroad."},{"time":"8:49","audioTime":529,"title":"Roanoke Island controlled Albemarle Sound","detail":"The island lay across the water route between Pamlico and Albemarle sounds and protected the approaches to northeast North Carolina and Norfolk."},{"time":"9:56","audioTime":596,"title":"Confederate defenses developed slowly","detail":"Benjamin Huger assigned troops after the loss of Hatteras, but command disputes and limited resources delayed construction."},{"time":"10:25","audioTime":625,"title":"Henry Wise took command in December","detail":"Confederate Secretary of War Judah P. Benjamin assigned Wise to the island late in 1861."},{"time":"11:06","audioTime":666,"title":"Wise found the garrison unprepared","detail":"About 1,500 soldiers were poorly trained, armed, clothed, and housed. The island’s artillery had inadequate ammunition and weak positions."},{"time":"11:47","audioTime":707,"title":"The garrison increased but Wise became ill","detail":"The force grew to about 2,500. Colonel Henry M. Shaw of the 8th North Carolina took field command when Wise was too sick to serve."},{"time":"12:22","audioTime":742,"title":"Burnside assembled more than 15,000 men","detail":"At Annapolis, he organized regiments from five northeastern states into brigades under John G. Foster, Jesse L. Reno, and John G. Parke."},{"time":"13:12","audioTime":792,"title":"Goldsborough organized the naval force","detail":"Twenty-one warships were to support the operation after it reached Hatteras. The transports were a varied group selected mainly because they drew less than eight feet of water."},{"time":"14:17","audioTime":857,"title":"The expedition left Annapolis","detail":"The transports departed January 9, met the naval force at Fort Monroe, and left Hampton Roads on January 11."},{"time":"14:36","audioTime":876,"title":"A storm scattered and damaged the fleet","detail":"Two gunboats and a transport sank, another transport went ashore and broke apart, and Burnside’s headquarters tug Picket nearly foundered."},{"time":"15:24","audioTime":924,"title":"The Hatteras bar was shallower than reported","detail":"Burnside had planned for eight feet of water but found about six. Several vessels deliberately grounded while current and tugs cut a deeper channel beneath them."},{"time":"16:09","audioTime":969,"title":"The force assembled inside Pamlico Sound","detail":"By February 5, the surviving fleet had crossed the bar. Fog delayed the final movement until February 7."},{"time":"17:45","audioTime":1065,"title":"The ships entered Croatan Sound","detail":"The expedition approached Ashby’s Harbor on the island’s west side. Thomas Robinson, a self-emancipated local teenager, had identified it as the best landing site."},{"time":"18:00","audioTime":1080,"title":"The Confederates had built several defenses","detail":"Forts Huger, Blanchard, and Bartow mounted 25 guns. A three-gun redoubt blocked the central road, and the floating Fort Forrest held seven guns near the mainland."},{"time":"18:47","audioTime":1127,"title":"Four regiments defended the island","detail":"The garrison included the 8th and 31st North Carolina and the 49th and 59th Virginia. Sunken vessels and pilings obstructed Croatan Sound."},{"time":"19:11","audioTime":1151,"title":"The naval engagement occupied February 7","detail":"Confederate gunboats fought from behind the obstructions and then withdrew. United States ships covered the army transports moving toward Ashby’s Harbor."},{"time":"19:43","audioTime":1183,"title":"Burnside began landing at 4 p.m.","detail":"Almost 4,000 men were ashore within an hour. By midnight, about 7,500 soldiers and a howitzer battery had landed south of the Confederate forts."},{"time":"20:17","audioTime":1217,"title":"Shaw blocked the island road","detail":"He placed about 1,000 infantry at the central earthwork. Rain soaked both armies during the night."},{"time":"20:38","audioTime":1238,"title":"The advance began at 8 a.m. on February 8","detail":"Fog, mud, felled trees, and a narrow road slowed the United States column."},{"time":"21:20","audioTime":1280,"title":"The redoubt had room for only part of the garrison","detail":"Shaw could place about 400 men at the breastworks. The remainder waited roughly 250 yards behind them and could not reinforce quickly."},{"time":"21:40","audioTime":1300,"title":"Foster led the direct attack","detail":"The 25th Massachusetts advanced along the road, Reno followed, and Parke remained in reserve."},{"time":"22:01","audioTime":1321,"title":"The frontal advance stopped","detail":"Confederate musketry and canister forced the 25th Massachusetts to the ground. The 10th Connecticut relieved it after about two hours."},{"time":"23:17","audioTime":1397,"title":"Both leading brigades entered the swamps","detail":"Foster sent the 23rd and 27th Massachusetts around the Confederate left. Reno sent three regiments around the right."},{"time":"24:45","audioTime":1485,"title":"Reno’s regiments reached the right flank","detail":"United States troops emerged from waist-deep swamp, drove off the nearest defenders, and fired along the earthwork."},{"time":"25:08","audioTime":1508,"title":"Foster’s men reached the other flank","detail":"The 23rd Massachusetts appeared on the Confederate left. Fire from both directions made the redoubt untenable."},{"time":"25:30","audioTime":1530,"title":"The 9th New York charged up the road","detail":"Rush Hawkins sent the regiment past the 10th Connecticut after the flanking attacks had already begun to break the Confederate line."},{"time":"26:46","audioTime":1606,"title":"Shaw surrendered the island","detail":"About thirty minutes after the earthwork fell, Shaw concluded that further resistance would only cause unnecessary losses."},{"time":"27:16","audioTime":1636,"title":"The victory cost fewer than 300 United States casualties","detail":"The episode gives army losses as 37 killed and 214 wounded and naval losses as 3 killed and 11 wounded."},{"time":"27:33","audioTime":1653,"title":"Most of the Confederate force became prisoners","detail":"The episode gives Confederate losses as 23 killed, 58 wounded, and 2,675 captured. Burnside paroled the prisoners because he could not hold them."},{"time":"28:07","audioTime":1687,"title":"Burnside prepared to attack New Bern","detail":"The capture of Roanoke Island opened Albemarle Sound. On March 11, Burnside embarked about 11,000 troops for the next operation."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"George B. McClellan","command":"General in chief","subordinates":"Set the expedition’s objectives in orders dated January 7"},{"commander":"Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"Coastal Division","subordinates":"More than 15,000 soldiers organized for amphibious operations"},{"commander":"John G. Foster","command":"1st Brigade","subordinates":"Led the road attack; 23rd and 27th Massachusetts turned the Confederate left"},{"commander":"Jesse L. Reno","command":"2nd Brigade","subordinates":"Sent three regiments through the swamp around the Confederate right"},{"commander":"John G. Parke","command":"3rd Brigade","subordinates":"Held initially in reserve"},{"commander":"Louis M. Goldsborough","command":"North Atlantic Blockading Squadron","subordinates":"Twenty-one naval vessels supported the landing and attacked the forts and Confederate gunboats"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Benjamin Huger","command":"Department of Norfolk","subordinates":"Held higher responsibility for the North Carolina coast and Roanoke Island"},{"commander":"Henry A. Wise","command":"District of Roanoke Island","subordinates":"Sought reinforcements and supplies but was ill during the battle"},{"commander":"Henry M. Shaw","command":"Roanoke Island field force","subordinates":"8th and 31st North Carolina; 49th and 59th Virginia; surrendered the island"},{"commander":"William F. Lynch","command":"Confederate naval force","subordinates":"Small gunboats operating behind obstructions in Croatan Sound"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Ambrose E. Burnside","role":"Organized and commanded the joint expedition that captured Roanoke Island","side":"United States","image":"/images/ambrose-burnside.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666399/"},{"name":"Louis M. Goldsborough","role":"Commanded the naval component and coordinated the February 7 bombardment and landing","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry A. Wise","role":"Confederate district commander who warned that the island lacked troops, arms, and supplies","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Henry M. Shaw","role":"Commanded the island’s defenders during Wise’s illness and surrendered on February 8","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas Robinson","role":"Self-emancipated Roanoke Island resident who gave Burnside information about defenses, channels, and the landing site","side":"United States"},{"name":"John G. Foster","role":"Led the frontal attack and the flanking movement against the Confederate left","side":"United States"},{"name":"Jesse L. Reno","role":"Directed the flanking movement through the swamp against the Confederate right","side":"United States"},{"name":"John G. Parke","role":"Commanded the expedition’s reserve brigade","side":"United States"},{"name":"Rush C. Hawkins","role":"Commanded the 9th New York and had promoted a larger North Carolina coastal campaign","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Washington Whitman","role":"51st New York soldier whose letter described Reno’s swamp movement","side":"United States"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/roanoke-island-landing.jpg","alt":"1862 lithograph of the Burnside expedition landing at Roanoke Island","caption":"A contemporary lithograph of Burnside’s troops landing on February 7. Surfboats moved soldiers from transports to the island.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2003655799/"},{"image":"/images/roanoke-island-capture-print.jpg","alt":"1862 print of the capture of Roanoke Island by Burnside and Goldsborough","caption":"An 1862 print combining scenes from the joint Army–Navy operation and the capture of the island.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/90714196/"},{"image":"/images/ambrose-burnside.jpg","alt":"Portrait of United States Brigadier General Ambrose Burnside","caption":"Ambrose Burnside organized the Coastal Division, assembled its improvised transport fleet, and commanded the landing and battle.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666399/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 109","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/109-the-burnside-expedition-part-the-first"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Roanoke Island","url":"https://home.nps.gov/fora/learn/historyculture/battleofroanokeisland.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Roanoke Island battle summary","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=nc002"},{"label":"National Park Service: Thomas Robinson","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/thomas-robinson.htm"},{"label":"Naval History and Heritage Command: Navy Civil War chronology","url":"https://www.history.navy.mil/research/library/online-reading-room/title-list-alphabetically/n/navy-civil-war-chronology.html"},{"label":"Naval History and Heritage Command: Burnside expedition images","url":"https://www.history.navy.mil/our-collections/photography/wars-and-events/the-american-civil-war--1861-1865/north-carolina-sounds/burnside-expedition-to-new-bern--february-1862.html"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Plan of the Battle of Roanoke Island","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00070/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Burnside expedition landing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2003655799/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Capture of Roanoke Island","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/90714196/"}]}}]}]
